ts um making sure you're  using the affordances that platforms do  have so using the mute block and report  buttons because those send important  signals to the platforms to tell them  what's going on and that perhaps this  person who's been sending abusive  messages all day long  needs to to endure some sort of  consequence with his or her account um  and then finally it talks about building  a community not just within your peers  and uh colleagues but you know looking  for support among employers it's not  just newsrooms that should be looking  out for their employees in this day and  age we have health care workers we have  you know academics we have lots and lots  of people who use the internet as part  of their jobs and employers need to do a  little bit more to make sure they're  supporting those folks  hello and welcome back to the daily  climate show on sky news let's discuss  some more of these crucial climate  issues with our guest today alyssa  gilbert director of policy and  translation at the grantham institute  and james streeter from the inter  climate network  so climate change huge issue affects  every area of our lives some people even  suffering with climate anxiety  one way to ease said climate anxiety is  to empower people james let's start with  you that's something that your  organization is working to to do  yes thanks chloe um  let me just explain very briefly  interclimate network is um a uk charity  where we engage young people at  secondary school level  uh and run events  throughout the year um last year we  actually carried out one of the largest  um surveys of young people on climate  change and sustainability  and um  you're absolutely right the the one of  the things we found was the the  challenge with uh  the anxiety  but um and this does come from uh as you  said this sort of powerlessness so it's  really important that action is  something which is promoted as part of  education  so i think the sustainability and  climate change