ork  we'll be discussing the disruptive  protests and whether it's really  effective also how good education might  be key to solving the climate crisis  [Music]  i mean everybody gets abuse online to  some degree and that's not acceptable  but the abuse that women get is often of  a sexualized engendered nature for  instance i'm often sent pictures of  empty egg cartons because i'm a woman in  my 30s making my voice heard in a  predominantly male industry and some men  think that i should stick to making  babies and often for women online abuse  becomes offline abuse very quickly  we are doxxed our personal information  is shared online often  here in the u.s we see swatting where a  swat team is called to someone's house  under a fake bomb threat women are  stalked um and i've had friends who are  active in this space who have had the  fbi show up to their house because there  are legitimate death threats against  them so yes you could learn a lot from  this book in terms of you know i.t  security and physical security but it  really is focused on the specific  threats that women face and how to make  sure that we have an equitable space  online because uh those who are abusing  us who are harassing us wish to silence  us frankly and that shouldn't be  acceptable in 2022 just as it wouldn't  be acceptable if these these insults  these slurs were being shouted at us on  the street or in the workplace well the  first thing i think is just very basic  i.t security making sure you're using  different passwords for all your  accounts you can use a password manager  to do this making sure you've got  two-factor authentication turned on so  that if somebody did get a hold of your  password they'd need another factor to  get into that account um that protects  your personal information and then  there's a little bit more tactical uh  you know tips on how to deal with trolls  online all the different sorts of people  that show up in our twitter mentions or  facebook comments um making sure you're  using the affordances that platforms do  have so using the mute block and report  buttons because those send important  signals to the platforms to tell them  what's going on and that perhaps this  person who's been sending abusive  messages all day long  needs to to endure some sort of  consequence with his or her account um  and then finally it talks about building  a community not just within your peers  and uh colleagues but you know looking  for support among employers it's not  just newsrooms that should be looking  out for their employees in this day and  age we have health care workers we have  you know academics we have lots and lots  of people who use the internet as part  of their jobs and employers need to do a  little bit more to make sure they're  supporting those folks  hello and welcome back to the daily  climate show on sky news let's discuss  some more of these crucial climate  issues with our guest today alyssa  gilbert director of policy and  translation at the grantham institute  and james streeter from the inter  climate network  so climate change huge issue affects  every area of our lives some people even  suffering with climate anxiety  one way to ease said climate anxiety is  to empower people james let's start with  you that's something that your  organization is working to to do  yes thanks chloe um  let me just explain very briefly  interclimate network is um a uk charity  where we engage young people at  secondary school level  uh and run events  throughout the year um last year we  actually carried out one of the largest  um surveys of young people on climate  change and sustainability  and um  you're absolutely right the the one of  the things we found was the the  challenge with uh  the anxiety  but um and this does come from uh as you  said this sort of powerlessness so it's  really important that action is  something which is promoted as part of  education  so i think the sustainability and  climate change strategy for education is  a good start and the natural history  gcse  will formalize um that  uh part of the education system  uh as opposed to what it's been in the  past which has been uh down to a few  selected individuals  alyssa this is really complicated stuff  there's no simple solutions to climate  change  when do you think we should start  educating youngsters about climate  tissue when's the right time  well i think we should work in the same  way that we do in educating people about  any kind of issue and we start teaching  people the basic principles of asking  questions around science for example at  a very young age to learn the skills and  the ways of thinking that they require  so that they can become talented and  sophisticated scientists at a university  and postgraduate level eventually or  stop somewhere in between and apply  those skills the same is true for  climate change actually working on  climate change issues actually involves  possibly different ways of thinking we  need people to be able to think across  different sectors flexibly solve  problems  those kind of skills we should be  building into education from an early  age  and additionally it's important that  people appreciate and feel  nature  and they understand the environment  around them so the kind of immersive  learning student led approaches all of  these things can be incorporated in the  curriculum from very early on  and i think it was just last week or so  the government published  sustainability and climate change  education strategy or white paper that  came out of the department for education  and that encompasses um aspects of  learning about climate change  right from early years all the way up to  university postgraduate and adult skills  james let's bring you in what sort of  response have you had from students that  you've been working with with the work  that you've been doing  yeah the response has been amazing so  firstly could i just sort of um add to  elissa's answer i think the um  the the  really key point in in education is the  secondary level because climate change  is a complicated subject you need the  students to be at a level where they can  understand and really react and work  with that complexity  and also at secondary level  um yeah  you know you are made by your teacher  and brought along by the school to do  things that you would not necessarily do  yourself when you get to university it  becomes rather self-selective so i think  the um the uh secondary uh education  part is absolutely crucial  now the gcse um i think is great um it's  a good start  but um it's really important that it  cuts across a whole range of subjects  climate change as we're seeing is not  just about any more the biology and the  geography it's about economics it's  about politics it's about engineering  design things like this  and and also um the the importance of of  bringing in the action what we found  um through our survey into climate  network  is that  young people um they their awareness is  increased um as lilly cold mentioned in  her interview that the awareness when  she started was was virtually nothing  but what they want to to know is what to  do uh around climate change for example  what to buy that actually is not  degrading the environment  secondly they um they want to know uh  they want leadership they want the  adults in the room and particularly the  teachers to to walk the talk and show  them what to do  and then i think that um  thirdly here there's um it's about the  buildings and making them actually fit  for the future um if i think if you put  all the schools in the country together  you get an area roughly two times the  size of birmingham so there's a huge  potential there for um you know  mitigation but also there are  opportunities i mean think of the  biodiversity opportunities for example  if if you manage to do something across  that whole area  alyssia just quickly on this um it's not  just the youngsters we've been talking a  lot about youngsters and students is it  it's the whole workforce really we need  to up skill so they can also work in  in climate issues how do we go about  doing that  yeah you're absolutely right it's really  important that we make sure that we have  the skills available and necessary to  actually deliver  the the net zero and resilient future  that we see ahead of ourselves one of  the ways that we can do that is by  making sure that our approach to  education across all ages including  adult education is tightly linked to our  development of the green skills agenda  um and actually the government has been  long promising a green jobs delivery  group which will build on work both on  climate education and on the green jobs  task force so that we can pull together  education with skills as soon as  possible  okay and now let's uh change subject  earlier we were talking about that poll  that found more than half of britain's  so they support the demands being made  by the group just stop oil but perhaps  not their methods um  james let's start with you on on protest  um there's a fine line isn't there  between a protest to make a point and  then criminal activity  yeah absolutely and i think first of all  people should you know we've got to make  it clear  people should be allowed to protest  and um  it is a really good way of democratizing  climate change but you you're spot on it  has to be peaceful and legal  um  unfortunately  what we're finding is it's increasingly  being driven by desperation um people  around see that they the leadership are  not making the decisions and taking the  country in the direction they want  so um i think it's really  it's really important that um that  people  see that protesting comes in different  forms it's not just about the physical  abstraction that we've seen there's also  about schools marching for climate and  also more sophisticated protests such as  a legal challenge  alyssa let's get 